## Color tokens

Every color in `apps/web` product UI comes from a token in
`apps/web/src/app/globals.css`. Components name a role. They never name a
value.

Three things are banned. Each has one replacement.

### 1. No opacity modifiers on color utilities

Do not write `bg-primary/55`, `text-muted-foreground/70`, `border-border/40`,
or `bg-primary/[0.04]`.

An opacity modifier invents a new color at the call site. Two components that
want the same tint pick different numbers, so one tint becomes six. The
opacity is also applied to whatever sits behind the element, which means the
same class produces a different rendered color on a card than on the page.

Use the step token instead. Each step is the base color composited once
against the page ground and then frozen as an opaque value, so it renders the
same shade on a card as on the page:

| Step | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `-tertiary` | border or divider tint |
| `-quaternary` | hover or pressed fill |
| `-quinary` | resting fill, badge background |

The four semantic ramps carry all three plus a `-foreground` for a label on the
solid fill. The `status-*` ramps carry the base and `-quaternary`, which is what
a badge needs: a vivid glyph and a tinted pill.

`--semantic-destructive` splits in two. `--semantic-destructive-solid` is the
fill that carries a near-white label, and `--semantic-destructive` is the red
that reads as text on the page. In dark mode those pull opposite ways, so one
token cannot do both: white on the text red measures 3.43, under the 4.5 floor.

The rule is absolute. A modifier on any colour utility is a bug, including on
the neutrals, and `color-tokens.test.ts` fails on it.

Where a surface genuinely has to show what sits behind it, the alpha lives in
the token, not at the call site:

| Token | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `--overlay` | modal and dialog scrim |
| `--surface-glass` | floating chip or control over content |
| `--surface-sticky` | header that sits on the page |
| `--scrim`, `--scrim-soft`, `--scrim-strong` | over user media, same in both themes |
| `--on-media`, `--on-media-muted` | label riding on a scrim |
| `--on-media-tertiary` | hairline, ring or border drawn on media |
| `--on-media-quaternary` | hover wash under a control on media |
| `--media-ground` | the opaque ground behind media, and ink on light media |
| `--hero-wash` | the gradient laid over the landing photograph |
| `--background-muted` | quieter label on an inverted (`bg-foreground`) surface |
| `--card-background-hover` | row hover on a card, which must not hide the row under it |
| `--ring-halo`, `--destructive-halo` | the soft glow outside a focus ring |

The `on-media` family is the one place a name has tripped people up. `-muted`
is a *label*, at 85% white. The hairline and the wash are `-tertiary` and
`-quaternary`, at 15% and 10%. Reading "muted" as "faint" paints an 85% white
pill and swallows the white icon on top of it.

```tsx
// Do not
<div className="border-semantic-success/20 bg-semantic-success/5" />

// Do
<div className="border-semantic-success-tertiary bg-semantic-success-quinary" />
```

If no step fits the role, add one to `globals.css` in **both** theme blocks and
name it for the role it serves. Do not reach for a modifier because adding a
token feels heavier. One token beats twenty call sites that disagree.

Opacity modifiers stay legal on non-color utilities and on genuinely
continuous animation values driven at runtime.

### 2. No raw Tailwind palette colors

Do not write `bg-emerald-500`, `text-amber-600`, `border-red-300`,
`text-gray-400`, `bg-black`, `text-white`.

The palette is a second color system running beside the tokens. It produced
two different greens for "succeeded" on adjacent screens, because one badge
reached for `emerald` and another for `green`.

Map the meaning to a semantic ramp: `--semantic-success`,
`--semantic-warning`, `--semantic-destructive`, `--semantic-info`. Map neutrals
to `--foreground`, `--muted-foreground`, `--border`, `--background`, `--card`.

`bg-black` and `text-white` count as raw palette too. A literal cannot follow
the theme, and for media UI, where the ground really is theme-independent, the
`--media-ground` and `--on-media` tokens say so by name.

`--border` and `--input` are two roles, not one value. `--border` is the quiet
divider at 1.61 against the page. `--input` is the control boundary at 3.0,
which is what WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.11 asks of a non-text control.

### 3. No hex, rgb, hsl or oklch literals in components

Do not write `#6400FF`, `style={{ color: "#00a4fa" }}`, or
`shadow-[0_1px_2px_rgba(0,0,0,0.05)]`.

A literal cannot follow the theme, so it is wrong in one of light or dark by
construction. It also silently forks the brand: `#6400FF` is `--primary`, and
a palette change now needs an edit in two places.

Read the token. Where a canvas or WebGL painter genuinely needs a numeric
value, resolve it from the CSS variable, or leave a comment naming the token
the literal mirrors so the two stay in step.

Third-party brand marks (`components/social-icons.tsx`) and user-facing color
pickers are data, not UI color. Those keep their literals.

### Adding a token

1. Check an existing ramp does not already serve the role. Nine greens at hue
   142 all meant "good" before this rule existed.
2. Declare it in **both** `:root` and `.dark`. A token declared in `@theme`
   cannot be themed.
3. Bridge it in the `@theme inline` block so Tailwind emits the utility. Both
   halves fail silently on their own: a token with no bridge emits no utility
   at all, and a bridge with no token emits `var(--missing)`, which falls back
   to `currentColor` and paints the loudest edge on the page. Neither shows up
   in a type check. The guard checks both.
4. Measure the pair it renders on: 4.5:1 for text, 3:1 for a border, icon,
   status dot, or focus ring.

### Guard

`apps/web/src/lib/utils/__tests__/color-tokens.test.ts` fails the build on
five things. Each is its own test case, and a call-site failure prints the
file, the line number, and the offending line:

1. A raw Tailwind palette class on any colour utility, `ring-offset` included.
2. An opacity modifier on any colour utility. The rule is absolute; the only
   exemption is a line height after a font size, and it is scoped to `text-`,
   because `shadow` takes the same size words and a real alpha.
3. A colour literal in a component.
4. A bridged token that nothing defines.
5. A token defined in one theme block but not the other.

A hex counts only when it is quote-delimited or sits inside an arbitrary
Tailwind value, so an issue number in a comment is not a color. The allowlist
is the set of places that cannot read a custom property: third-party brand
marks, the color picker's own swatches, two canvas painters and a canvas
fallback that reads `--primary` first, the error page that renders when the
stylesheet failed, an iframe `srcdoc`, and the two surfaces the OS reads
rather than CSS. It is named file by file, and it exempts literals only. No
file is exempt from the palette rule.

The debt this rule was written against is gone. `apps/web/src` carried 820
opacity modifiers across 242 files and 192 raw palette classes across 40 files
when the sweep started; both are zero. The 55 literal lines that remain all sit
in the nine allowlisted files. Nothing here is aspirational any more, so a new
modifier is not debt to be tolerated, it is a build failure.
